A follow-up with some additional information.  I made a univariate variable, x, with 2000 specimens and performed the following three assessments:

> Y <- matrix(rnorm(2000*21000), 2000, 21000)
> x <- rnorm(2000)
> library(RRPP)
> system.time(lm.rrpp(Y ~ x, iter = 0, turbo = TRUE, Parallel = TRUE))
    user   system  elapsed
1164.072   10.457 1182.479
> system.time(lm.rrpp(Y ~ x, iter = 9, turbo = TRUE, Parallel = TRUE))
   user  system elapsed
821.904   8.503 831.072
> system.time(lm.rrpp(Y ~ x, iter = 99, turbo = TRUE, Parallel = TRUE))
   user  system elapsed
846.537  22.958 841.136

So strangely, the system time was longer for 0 iterations (almost 20 minutes) but was reduced to just under 14 minutes with 10 iterations, then only just over 14 minutes with 100 iterations.  This was on a MacBook Pro with an 8-core processor, 64GB RAM.

The point here is that after the overhead is completed, the RRPP operation is quite fast.  Your 30-minute time for 0 iterations might mean only a couple hours for 1000 RRPP iterations, even without a super computer (but a high-performance machine will help).

What a world we live in, if the hope for a data analysis like yours is one hour duration or less on a home computer!  There might be some in this group who can remember waiting half a day to get results from a simple analysis from a mainframe computer, and it does not seem like it was that long ago.

Mike

