>
>
> Is it because in the 2B-pPLS we take the phylogenetic relationship of the different primates into account?

Yes.

One might say that the significant (not really positive even if the line looks like that) relationship when not taking phylogeny into account is spurious, because the observations are not independent.  The expectation is that species with more recent evolutionary divergence have more correlated shapes, so accounting for that auto-correlation in a PGLS analysis is why the result is less correlated.  An alternative explanation is that evolutionary divergence is constrained along an integrated axis of shape and function.  Anatomical changes that result in improper function are not likely to evolve.   Accounting for phylogeny in 2B-PLS might reveal the subtle, novel evolutionary directions species take, independent of the anatomical constraint.  In your data set, 2B-PLS done without phylogeny might reveal a strong shape-function association that permeates all species, and 2B-PLS, after accounting for that association, might reveal the more unique evolutionary divergences independent of the association.

Some published work here <https://watermark.silverchair.com/evolut2580.pdf?token=AQECAHi208BE49Ooan9kkhW_Ercy7Dm3ZL_9Cf3qfKAc485ysgAAAsgwggLEBgkqhkiG9w0BBwagggK1MIICsQIBADCCAqoGCSqGSIb3DQEHATAeBglghkgBZQMEAS4wEQQMJxJJWDXt8o86lVzfAgEQgIICe84ruVxR4sOdR4zFgNKpV0JtSuqflMeb0CpgwwSx6iRromQaQXUcW8v5nU1NvK-eV1DXvPyGwzPwnsbdcUui7iqkef0h_TfmOSIpi7gV1BDvOANO78i2ov4mVRwYcU3CoKxl-VNJ5mkeBej-jW0l-OS_1RBwijGgIY-7kfQZIbBXI7FtI6ILqqL53TOSIe__9sDunpKgL47JHBOCX8PZygJjN1oTGRRYqSUQ9YrDoC6KZMe5xrKWRpZmGO6oPRWYogKndjlgcuY6S883XJqD_Qx2ZHa2YZ12y2MAS40TsUk6KRxxVOradpelm9Y1hQyU2Vgatm03DNVBq5WUR3q3XQNaSKuGYJ_QbmvhKpxfvJaeZhrjCYZIABCSIJ0rSpCcEoxrr6wfuw0s7f-dXhbiWWfG0gShRmc6RSTPDj_2EmanMng82mDzJ-RxDvbltpt6b3dacysLVWQoYaZ5NG4RHCD3wsRrM_lbDmq6v7i7mmyN8xtXCpIfbtwf6QVRWIdsHkd2YZ6pNSzUKPp-HK69RfFmYwMw9p7qspDOx3zOkn7WB2d8CoNFN1GLqQ4mcgy1mM7cFlJKh5V3sJg3miD1CYPAaexGtfW6uHaUfdKuRI0UtNim1w4vzpP5yWXyL4wehe1WH7xfRvEMFQ9ZkHjJ5sCQGQSLPsJo5MJBgfOVwFkPNZfyh7vYLowfMG9HPw8X482ZL1Sxl5mpVRPrEkjW4LQyvxgmCgzB8d_OXj56UIXgiQ4_NWDMD8wuypiwyxhUVFG3dG8b-jPtbfxZNbaGRv9RJdB3mzvUSf6rYyPfktTtJURROn8ePcctOAot5BEpsJz6yyGcdxVHwkDg> and here <https://www.researchgate.net/publication/367021738_Simultaneous_integration_and_modularity_underlie_the_exceptional_body_shape_diversification_of_characiform_fishes#fullTextFileContent> might help to elucidate more about what I am getting at, although these works are more focused on the morphological modules.

